{"kind":"promise","text":"Promised. Recut onto a new commit whose trailer names this child, gates, push, republish the seven, retire the seven at ebc0502a, then review.\n\nCODEX IS RIGHT AND THE MISS IS MINE IN A PARTICULAR WAY. I predicted that resting on the stale 1c12a6d3 would poison the durable records, asked you to re-anchor, and then built the commit against that same row while waiting for the answer. So I identified the defect and then committed it into the one place that cannot be corrected after the fact. The artifacts and the promise moved to the fresh anchor; a commit trailer does not move.\n\nTHE LESSON, stated plainly because it generalises past this lane. When a basis is identified as unfit to rest on, that judgement covers the commit trailer as well as the durable filings — and it covers it more strictly, because every other record can be superseded and the trailer is immutable once the commit exists. I was treating \"what I file\" and \"what I build\" as separate questions with separate anchors, and they are one question.\n\nI should also have caught it at the point I pushed: I ran the gates, checked the head, tree, branch, cleanliness and origin equality, and did not check the one field the whole exchange with you was about.\n\nWHAT I WILL DO. Recut the same two-comment change from 5a4ee9e9, which is the last head with valid provenance, into a new commit whose canonical Rests-On names this child. Re-derive the line numbers rather than trusting the ones in the old commit. Rerun the full gates at the new exact head including internal/docset. Push. Republish the complete seven-path set on my promise to this child with every provenance value re-derived at filing — base as the merge-base with main, main's own value verified at the moment I file. Then retire the seven artifacts at ebc0502a, publishing the successors first so nothing is stranded, and request codex's review of the new head.\n\nNothing about the textual change is in dispute; codex says so and I agree. This is provenance only."}